WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.367
This morning.

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:01.201
Florida Governor Ron

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.502
DeSantis is in New York

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.203
where he spoke

00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:04.471
at a rally there

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:06.873
supporting law enforcement

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:10.243
We support strong policies

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:11.978
to keep our communities safe

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.413
and we back the blue

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.048
and we support their mission.

00:00:15.048 --> 00:00:16.716
And we back it up with money

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:17.717
and all kinds of stuff

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:19.052
to be able to do it.

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:20.720
The reason why you have

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.022
what you have in some of these

00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:23.423
other jurisdictions

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:25.892
is because they're putting woke ideology

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:27.961
ahead of your safety

00:00:27.961 --> 00:00:30.830
as New Yorkers or as people from Illinois

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:31.664
or wherever.

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:34.667
You have this type of policy taking place

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:36.469
where U.S.

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:37.837
police NYPD do

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:38.905
say that they need better

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.040
gun safety measures

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.041
as well to fight crime.

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:42.142
The government has not announced

00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:44.344
that he is running for president.

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:45.678
He seemed to be courting,

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:46.980
however, New York voters this morning.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.580
His visit comes

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.382
as some conservatives are concerned

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:50.450
about how his fantasies

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.652
using his government powder in Florida

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:53.553
in his fight against what

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:55.021
he calls their wokeness.

00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:57.057
And Steve King joins us now.

00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:58.825
Steve, what specifically are

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:00.226
Republican leaders worried about

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:02.762
Jim, there are a couple of

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:03.830
factors at play here.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.533
First is you have conservative donors and

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:09.602
and organizations that generally believe

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:10.470
that government

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:11.204
shouldn't be

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:12.972
in the business of businesses

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:15.275
and that some of the steps taken

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:16.042
by Governor

00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:19.179
Santos have inflicted his conservative

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:21.848
ideology on state institutions.

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:23.083
And businesses

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:24.117
and sometimes even

00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:25.351
punishing businesses

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:27.220
that run afoul of his point of view.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.655
We've seen, obviously,

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:29.589
that has played out

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:30.490
publicly in his fight

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.625
with Disney over the so-called

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:34.394
don't say gay bill,

00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:35.728
but we've also seen him

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:37.664
punish businesses for their vaccination

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:39.332
and mask policies.

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:40.967
We've seen him put restrictions

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:43.036
on how businesses can train

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:45.038
their employees around issues of racism.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.473
We've seen him now

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:47.874
say he wants to go after banks

00:01:47.874 --> 00:01:49.442
that lend in ways

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.810
that he doesn't agree with.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.245
So there's that part of it.

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:53.947
But there's also Republicans,

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:55.248
including his supporters,

00:01:55.248 --> 00:01:55.748
who are worried

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:56.950
that he might be flying

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.918
a little bit too close to the sun

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.787
with his war on wokeness.

00:02:00.787 --> 00:02:02.422
You know, this most recent spat

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:04.324
with the College Board over this

00:02:04.324 --> 00:02:06.459
AP African-American studies class.

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:07.260
I mean,

00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:09.462
he is now talking about potentially

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:10.864
pulling AP classes

00:02:10.864 --> 00:02:12.465
out of Florida entirely,

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:14.601
finding alternatives to the S.A.T..

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:16.503
These are things

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:18.505
that parents around the country use

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:19.405
to help their students

00:02:19.405 --> 00:02:20.473
get into good colleges.

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.075
To save a bit of money

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:23.910
on their higher education.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:24.911
And there are concerns

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:26.079
that they don't know

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:27.747
how this is going to play out nationally

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:28.314
when this stuff

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.049
when he starts campaigning

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:31.618
in a national campaign.

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:32.552
And what we're seeing is

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:33.753
Republican candidates

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:35.321
starting to seize on this a bit,

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.456
especially those

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:37.157
who are starting

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:38.925
to want to turn the page

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:40.960
from these Trump era politics.

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:41.995
Are we seeing Governor

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:43.863
Sununu of New Hampshire,

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:44.764
former Governor

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:46.032
Hogan of Maryland,

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:47.367
Governor Hutchinson of Arkansas,

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:48.902
they have all raised concern

00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:51.204
with how the stances has been so heavy

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:52.172
handed with businesses.

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:53.072
Now, Jim,

00:02:53.106 --> 00:02:54.541
Governor Sanchez remains

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:56.075
very popular in Florida.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:57.443
He's growing popularity

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:58.711
across the country.

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:00.780
He has quite a bit of support

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.149
among those who want to see him challenge

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:04.117
President Donald Trump.

00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:05.251
But these are the questions

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.419
he is going to face

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:07.887
from Republican voters

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:09.822
and from his potential

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:10.490
competitors

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.559
if he does jump into that race.

00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:14.160
Steve King, thanks so much.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:14.661
Joining us now,

00:03:14.661 --> 00:03:15.061
Molly Ball,

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:16.196
a national political correspondent

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:17.764
for Time magazine, Somali.

00:03:17.764 --> 00:03:18.898
The conventional wisdom,

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:20.266
which I generally hate,

00:03:20.266 --> 00:03:20.800
had been

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:21.601
to some degree

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:23.369
that DeSantis was

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:24.904
the kind of anti-Trump candidate,

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:26.973
someone who could get the MAGA voters

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.942
to to back him.

00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:31.110
But not be Trump in effect.

00:03:31.144 --> 00:03:31.611
You would hear that

00:03:31.611 --> 00:03:32.512
from some Republicans,

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:33.580
but to hear some conservatives

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:34.380
now concerned

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.683
that DeSantis, his positions in Florida,

00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:38.851
might be too out there,

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:40.486
what are we seeing within the Republican

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:41.254
Party right now?

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:44.490
Well, I think

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:44.824
a lot

00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:45.258
of it is

00:03:45.258 --> 00:03:46.926
that without an official candidacy

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:47.860
by DeSantis,

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.862
a lot of this is just filling a vacuum

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:52.365
with sort of speculation and rumor.

00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:52.599
Look,

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:53.399
a lot of the things

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:54.634
that Republican voters

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:56.102
really like about Governor

00:03:56.102 --> 00:03:58.171
DeSantis are the similarities with Trump,

00:03:58.471 --> 00:03:59.639
chiefly his willingness

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:00.173
to use

00:04:00.173 --> 00:04:02.275
the force of the state, state coercion,

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:03.643
in some cases

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:04.844
to punish businesses

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:06.846
and to enforce the sort of

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:09.115
culture war initiatives

00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:10.283
that he has his eye on.

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:10.883
And so that is,

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.752
I think, viewed as a strength

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:14.821
by many of the Sanders's fans.

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:16.689
And it's the sort of similarity

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:18.858
with Trump he's been able to.

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:19.492
And I think this is

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:20.159
also a sign

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:21.461
of how the Republican Party

00:04:21.461 --> 00:04:23.263
has sort of realigned itself

00:04:23.263 --> 00:04:24.931
ideologically around the Trump

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:26.633
axis since Trump first

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:27.600
became a candidate.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.335
Now, nearly a decade ago,

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:30.069
these concerns

00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:31.771
about the size and scope of government

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:32.505
and the sort of

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:34.274
ideological libertarianism

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:35.441
that used to underlie

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:37.210
a lot of Republican doctrine

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.279
is really not as much on people's minds

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:40.146
as it used to be.

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:41.547
And so a lot of the candidates

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:42.348
who are

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:44.851
more overtly against the sort of legacy

00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:46.419
that Trump has left in the party are,

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:48.288
of course, going to object to that.

00:04:48.288 --> 00:04:49.856
But I think that is one of the things

00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:51.190
that for many Republican base

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.159
voters is a strength for DeSantis.

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:53.726
Yeah, interesting.

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:53.993
You note

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:54.727
it is eight years

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:56.462
since that ride down the escalator, four

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:57.463
for Trump.

00:04:57.597 --> 00:04:58.731
I want to play something

00:04:58.731 --> 00:04:59.432
that Nikki Haley,

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:00.133
of course, has already

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:02.402
announced her candidacy for 20, 24

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:03.136
said during her

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:05.104
travels through Iowa this week

00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:07.240
regarding Social Security and Medicare.

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:07.774
Have a listen.

00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:08.708
I want to get your reaction

00:05:10.843 --> 00:05:11.277
There is

00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:12.478
an issue of entitlements,

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:14.147
and I think that we do have to address

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:15.048
entitlements.

00:05:15.048 --> 00:05:17.250
However, I don't think we take away

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:18.184
from anyone

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:19.619
that we've promised things to.

00:05:19.619 --> 00:05:21.721
I think we focus on the new generation

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:23.489
coming into the system

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:24.490
because they already know

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:24.991
they're not going

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:26.192
to get anything from it.

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:28.194
And we need to get in front of that.

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:29.529
And that means you go

00:05:29.529 --> 00:05:30.930
and you look at the new ones coming in

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.498
and you keep your promises to those

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:35.101
that have already been in the system.

00:05:35.101 --> 00:05:35.802
I listen to that.

00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:37.337
That sounds a lot to me

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:38.971
like saying, okay,

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:40.306
you're already receiving Medicare,

00:05:40.306 --> 00:05:41.607
Social Security, you won't be touched.

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:43.242
But the promise to those of us

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:45.178
who are paying right now

00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:46.212
may not be capped.

00:05:46.212 --> 00:05:46.612
Right.

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:48.848
I mean, that sounds to me like

00:05:48.848 --> 00:05:50.283
entitlements are on the table.

00:05:50.283 --> 00:05:51.351
And this is a candidate

00:05:51.351 --> 00:05:52.352
for for the Republican

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:53.586
nomination in 20, 24

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:57.623
Well, and again, I feel like

00:05:57.623 --> 00:05:59.859
I'm having flashbacks to 2015. Right.

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:02.061
I mean this is exactly sort of the

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:02.895
the Paul Ryan

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:04.697
line of the old Republican Party,

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:06.032
which was very determined

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:08.067
to enact some sort of entitlement reform.

00:06:08.301 --> 00:06:09.402
And it is still the case

00:06:09.402 --> 00:06:10.203
that a lot of fiscal

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:11.838
conservatives, party donors,

00:06:11.838 --> 00:06:14.006
sort of Old-School, Republican types

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:14.574
who believe

00:06:14.574 --> 00:06:16.075
that the spending on these programs

00:06:16.075 --> 00:06:16.976
is unsustainable

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:19.178
and there does have to be some reform.

00:06:19.178 --> 00:06:20.079
And it has always been

00:06:20.079 --> 00:06:20.480
the case

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:21.647
that these sort of plans

00:06:21.647 --> 00:06:23.883
to reform entitlements for the most part

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:25.985
have targeted future beneficiaries,

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:27.553
not current beneficiaries

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:29.522
who are already receiving

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:31.891
or who are potentially about to receive

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:33.359
these benefits.

00:06:33.359 --> 00:06:35.161
But it is obviously something that Trump

00:06:35.161 --> 00:06:36.963
has made a litmus test for the party

00:06:36.963 --> 00:06:37.597
and has made

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:39.232
a dividing line in the party,

00:06:39.232 --> 00:06:40.466
and that we have seen

00:06:40.466 --> 00:06:41.701
become a source of tension

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:43.202
in the party between, for example,

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:45.238
Mitch McConnell and Rick Scott,

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:46.506
because these entitlement programs

00:06:46.506 --> 00:06:48.908
are incredibly, incredibly popular.

00:06:49.108 --> 00:06:49.876
You know, 80

00:06:49.876 --> 00:06:51.010
90% of voters

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:52.645
do not want to see any changes

00:06:52.645 --> 00:06:54.313
to Social Security

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:55.882
They're just viewed as a guarantee

00:06:55.882 --> 00:06:56.549
and something that

00:06:56.549 --> 00:06:57.450
that many Americans

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.152
feel like they've earned.

00:06:59.152 --> 00:06:59.752
So, you know,

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:00.520
that was a big part

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.990
of Trump's success in 2016 and

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.492
and I think it's something that again

00:07:06.492 --> 00:07:08.194
the party has sort of realigned itself

00:07:08.194 --> 00:07:10.129
around to the point where it's become

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:10.663
it's always been

00:07:10.663 --> 00:07:11.631
sort of a third rail

00:07:11.631 --> 00:07:12.832
and it's become something

00:07:12.832 --> 00:07:14.967
that is seen as sort of untouchable.

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:15.868
So this is going to be,

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:17.470
I think, a live debate in the party

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.572
in the primaries this year. Yeah.

00:07:19.572 --> 00:07:20.973
They're not describing it as untouchable.

00:07:20.973 --> 00:07:22.074
And by the way,

00:07:22.141 --> 00:07:23.476
we're all paying into it now right

00:07:23.476 --> 00:07:24.510
just watch your paycheck

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:25.144
every two weeks

00:07:25.144 --> 00:07:26.279
as those contributions

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:27.780
still go in, whatever the discussions.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:28.915
Molly Ball, thanks so much.

